--TEST--
`::class` on a class side that is not statically known is `E0702`, and on a name that resolves to nothing is `E0303` — never a panic and never a folded guess (ADR 0011, ADR 0008)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0011 puts every reflective question on `Core\Reflect`: an object
// carries no name a program can read back, so a receiver's runtime class
// is not something `::class` can answer.
class User {
}
User $u = new User();
echo $u::class;

// ADR 0008 binds `static` to whichever class the call was made on, so
// folding it here would answer the declaring class instead — silently a
// different string rather than an absent one, which is the reason this
// one is refused and `self::class` is not.
class Base {
    public static function name(): string {
        return static::class;
    }
}

// A name that resolves to nothing is the same mistake, and the same code,
// `new Undeclared()` already takes. PHP folds this to `"Bogus"` because
// the string is on its way to `new $name`; ADR 0011 leaves it nowhere to
// go, so the typo has no later place to be caught.
echo Bogus::class;
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0702]: `::class` needs a class named at compile time
  --> %s:8:6
  |
8 | echo $u::class;
  |      ^^ this names no class the compiler can resolve
  = help: write the class itself — `Foo::class`, `self::class` — or take the question to the type system. An object carries no name a program can read back: ADR 0011 puts every reflective question on `Core\Reflect`

error[E0702]: `::class` needs a class named at compile time
   --> %s:16:16
   |
16 |         return static::class;
   |                ^^^^^^ `static` is not known until the call runs
   = help: ADR 0008 binds `static` to whichever class the call was made on, so folding it here would answer the declaring class instead — write `self::class` if that is what was meant

error[E0303]: `Bogus` is not declared
   --> %s:24:6
   |
24 | echo Bogus::class;
   |      ^^^^^ no matching declaration

error: aborting due to 3 errors
